        }
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Warm half the olive oil in the stockpot over a medium heat. Add the onion and cook for 8 minutes until soft and translucent. Add the carrot and celery and cook for another 3 minutes."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Stir in the tomato puree and cook for 2 minutes so it darkens slightly and loses its raw edge."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Add the drained chickpeas and bay leaves. Pour in enough cold water to cover by about 5cm. Bring to the boil."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Reduce the heat to a gentle simmer. Cover partially and cook for 75 to 90 minutes until the chickpeas are completely tender and the soup has thickened. Top up with hot water if it looks too thick during cooking."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Remove the bay leaves. Season well with salt and black pepper. Stir in the remaining olive oil and the chopped parsley. Serve in deep bowls with crusty bread."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "attrs": {
          "items": [
            {
              "problem": "The chickpeas are still firm after 90 minutes",
              "solution": "Old dried pulses take longer to cook. Make sure you soaked them overnight. If they are still firm, continue simmering and add more water as needed."
            },
            {
              "problem": "The soup is too thin",
              "solution": "Remove the lid for the last 20 minutes to let it reduce, or lightly mash some of the chickpeas against the side of the pot to thicken the broth."
            },
            {
              "problem": "The soup lacks depth",
              "solution": "The full quantity of olive oil is not optional here. It carries the flavour of the whole dish. Do not reduce it."
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Greeks consider "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "glossaryTooltip",
                "attrs": {
                  "termSlug": "fasolada-term"
                }
              }
            ],
            "text": "fasolada"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " the national dish, which speaks to how central it has been to everyday cooking for centuries. It is peasant food in the best sense: cheap ingredients transformed by long, slow heat and good olive oil into something deeply satisfying. It is traditionally eaten during the fasting periods of the Orthodox calendar. A bowl of it with bread is a complete meal."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
